{"text":"SASRec’s training objective is a plausible bottleneck, but does not establish the cause of weak Electronics ranking. [gSASRec (IJCAI 2024 extended abstract; original RecSys 2023)](https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2024/939) identifies overconfidence from negative-sampled BCE and introduces generalized BCE. Its original study also compares sampled softmax with 16 negatives. This supports testing both negative count and loss calibration; simply increasing score margins is insufficient evidence of better generalization.

Hard-negative contrastive learning offers another direction. [NARec (2025)](https://ietresearch.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1049/aie2.70004) combines hard negatives from batch/global pools with learned negative augmentation. [SID4SRec (2026)](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0950705126010221) explicitly filters semantically overlapping negatives at sequence and item levels. Its category/brand features are unavailable here, so the complete method cannot transfer directly. These results motivate cautious hard-negative mining with false-negative controls, rather than assuming the highest-scoring unobserved items are reliable negatives.

Practical experimental inference: compare unchanged SASRec with 16–64 uniform negatives under sampled-softmax cross-entropy, multi-negative BCE, and gBCE; then test a small hard-negative mixture. Exclude padding, target collisions, and known training-history positives; use training histories only for filtering. Treat negative counts and mixture proportions as hypotheses. Select using the pinned four-category macro validation HR@10 and inspect Electronics separately. [Krichene and Rendle](https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2021/0651.pdf) show sampled metrics can reverse full-catalog model rankings, so retain the required 99-negative evaluator and interpret improvements specifically under that protocol."}
